<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Homework 5 (Weeks 8 and 9)</title>

  <page>
  

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
            Recall that if <m>f</m> is a wave function, then <m>f^2</m> is a probability distribution.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the wave function
                    <m>f(\rho, \theta, \varphi) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{a_0^3\pi}}e^{-\rho/a_0}</m>, set up the appropriate triple integral 
            <m>\iiint_S f^2(\rho, \theta, \varphi) \quad dV</m> which would determine whether <m>f^2</m> is normalized.
            Then compute the integral.
                    (This is a wave function 
                    for a <m>1s</m> H orbital.)
                </p>
                <p>
                You will need to use the fact that <m>\int_0^\infty x^n e^{-bx} \quad dx = \frac{n!}{b^{n+1}}</m>.
            </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the wave function 
                  <m>f(\rho, \theta, \varphi) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{32a_0^3\pi}}\frac{\rho}{a_0}e^{-\rho/(2a_0)}\sin(\varphi)\cos(\theta)</m>, 
                  set up the appropriate triple integral 
            <m>\iiint_S f^2(\rho, \theta, \varphi) \quad dV</m> which would determine whether <m>f^2</m> is normalized.
                  (This is a wave function 
                    for a <m>2p_x</m> H orbital.)
                </p>
                <p>
                 It is an optional challenge to compute the integral, but not required.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
        Let 
        <md>
            \psi_1(\theta,\varphi) = \sqrt{\frac{3}{4\pi}}\cos(\varphi)
        </md>
        and 
        <md>
            \psi_2(\theta,\varphi) = \sqrt{\frac{5}{16\pi}}(3\cos^2(\varphi)-1)
        </md>.

        
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Set up a double integral in spherical coordinates which would be used to determine whether 
                   <m>\psi_1</m> and <m>\psi_2</m> are orthogonal functions over the surface of the unit sphere. Use the standard 
                   definition of an inner product of functions from class.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use Desmos to compute your integral from the previous part, and make a conclusion about whether 
                    or not these functions are orthogonal over the surface of the unit sphere.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Using the wave function from part 1(a), <m>f(\rho, \theta, \varphi) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{a_0^3\pi}}e^{-\rho/a_0}</m>,
                compute the probability that an electron is within a sphere of radius <m>r</m> centered at the nucleus. 
            </p>
            <p>To do this,
                first set up the appropriate integral in the order <q><m>d\theta d\varphi d\rho</m></q>. Then reduce it to a single <q><m>d\rho</m></q> integral
                using the fundamental theorem of calculus. Finally use technology to evaluate the <q><m>d\rho</m></q> integral. 
            </p>
            <p>
                Note that <m>a_0</m> is the <term>Bohr radius</term>, <m>a_0 \approx 5.29 \times 10^{-11}</m> m.
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>r= a_0/2</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
         <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>r= a_0</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
         <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>r= 2a_0</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
         <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Between <m>r= 0.9a_0</m> and <m>r= 1.1a_0</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

   
        <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
             In the previous problem, the final function of <m>\rho</m> that you should have been integrating is 
             <m>f(\rho) = \frac{4}{a_0^3} \rho^2 e^{-2\rho/a_0}</m>. This is known as the <term>radial probability distribution</term>.
             Use it to compute the expected value of the radius (your answer can be left as an expression involving the constant <m>a_0</m>).
            
        </p>
        <p>
                You will need to use the fact that <m>\int_0^\infty x^n e^{-bx} \quad dx = \frac{n!}{b^{n+1}}</m>.
            </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>\vvec_1 = \langle 2, 4 \rangle</m> and <m>\vvec_2 = \langle -1,-4 \rangle</m>      .    
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch each position vector.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>\wvec = -2\vvec_1 + 3\vvec_2</m>. This is called a <term>linear combination</term> of the 
                    vectors <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>. Sketch <m>\wvec</m>, illustrating how it is obtained from
                    the original vectors <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute and sketch the displacement vector from <m>\vvec_1</m> to <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute the angle <m>\theta</m> (<m>-\pi \lt \theta \leq \pi</m>) which specifies the direction 
                    of the vector <m>\vvec_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>|\vvec_2|</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Normalize <m>\vvec_1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>\vvec_1 = \langle 1,5\rangle</m>. Find all unit vectors perpendicular to <m>\vvec_1</m> using the
                dot product.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Vectors <m>\vvec</m> and <m>\wvec</m> lie in the <m>x-y</m> plane shown below. Sketch and label both
                <m>\vvec \times \wvec</m> and <m>\wvec \times \vvec</m>, and write a short explanation. Vector magnitudes
                need not be accurate, but the directions should be.
            </p>
            <image source="cross.png">
                <shortdescription>Two vectors in the x,y plane in 3 dimensions</shortdescription>
            </image>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    </page>


</worksheet>
